---
/*
  DRAFT — NOT ROUTED, NOT PUBLISHED.

  Underscore prefix keeps this file out of the build. This is the reserved slot
  for a Field notes essay: structure and stance only, no prose yet, no numbers.

  Governing rules (portfolio-kit/03-field-notes-essay.md):
    · No measured numbers. Any figure must be arithmetic from a stated assumption.
    · Name Petals, exo, llama.cpp RPC, distributed-llama, PipeDream at least once.
    · No "novel", "first", "state of the art".
    · End with one link: the ColabSwarm lab line, or its case file once it exists.
    · 1,500–2,500 words, one idea per section, no bullet lists in the body.

  When it is ready: rename to why-p2p-is-scheduling.astro, add a "Field notes"
  entry beside the Lab, and set noindex until the first draft stops moving.
*/
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Colophon from '../../components/Colophon.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Why P2P inference on free GPUs is mostly a scheduling problem — Amarthya T."
  description="Draft essay. The hard part of pooling free GPUs is not splitting the model or moving tensors, but paying for failure."
>
  <Nav />

  <main id="main" class="wrap note">
    <p class="note__status">Draft · outline only · not published</p>

    <h1 class="display">Why P2P inference on free GPUs is mostly a scheduling problem</h1>

    <p class="note__standfirst">
      The hard part of pooling free GPUs is not splitting the model or moving tensors — it is
      deciding what to do when a node disappears without warning, and paying for that decision in
      recomputation.
    </p>

    <ol class="note__outline">
      <li><span class="eyebrow">01</span> The appeal — free GPUs are abundant and idle; the bottleneck is coordination under churn, not compute.</li>
      <li><span class="eyebrow">02</span> The three bandwidths — compute, memory, network, and which one binds at each phase.</li>
      <li><span class="eyebrow">03</span> The number that decides the architecture — model bytes against per-hop payload, and why decode sends activations.</li>
      <li><span class="eyebrow">04</span> Failure is the dominant term — preemption arithmetic, against GPipe's and PipeDream's datacenter assumptions.</li>
      <li><span class="eyebrow">05</span> What I would measure — recovery latency, recomputed work, resume rate, output equivalence; why "tokens lost" is the wrong headline.</li>
      <li><span class="eyebrow">06</span> What I built — one factual paragraph, present tense, no results.</li>
      <li><span class="eyebrow">07</span> What this means for scheduling — placement as a scheduling problem with a failure model; redundancy as a budget.</li>
    </ol>
  </main>

  <Colophon />
</Base>

